.TH MARSHALTEST 1
.SH NAME
marshaltest \- regression test for marshal(1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B marshaltest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Marshaltest
loads
.BR /p9/dis/marshal.dis 's
in-process test seams and checks them two different ways, matching
the two very different pieces of
.IR marshal (1)
this suite covers.
.PP
The RFC822 address tokenizer and alias-expansion engine
.RB ( get822token() / expandline() )
is checked via
.B testexpandline()
against expectations that were themselves verified, during
development, against a standalone oracle harness built with this
backlog's usual
.B #C
+
.B clang
pattern - but every function body in that harness was copied
.B verbatim
from the real
.B marshal.c
(only a driver
.B main()
and an in-memory alias-list stub, replacing
.BR readaliases() 's
own real file read, are new), since
.B marshal.c
itself cannot be built standalone the way
.IR qr.c (1)
or
.IR cb.c (1)
can - it needs the rest of
.B upas/common
(mbox paths, folder locking) merely to link, none of which the
address engine itself ever calls. That harness was not kept in this
repository and is not run automatically here - see BUGS.
.PP
The composition engine
.RB ( run() ,
covering header assembly, MIME multipart structure, base64 encoding,
and attachment handling) has no such oracle at all - there is nothing
to build standalone here, since composing a message IS marshal's own
job, not a subset delegated to a library the way
.IR iconv (1)'s
pixel conversion was. These cases are checked by hand-tracing the
real source's own algorithm against
.BR checkcontains / checknotcontains
assertions on the composed output, plus one real byte-for-byte
base64 round-trip
.RB ( t21 :
encode a known binary attachment, decode the result back out of the
composed message, compare against the original bytes exactly).
.SH CASES
.TP
.I t1
-
.I t16
The address engine - see this file's own header comment in the
source for the full case list and the real findings they turned up
(a stale C comment, and a genuinely non-converging self-referential
alias bounded only by
.BR rexpand() 's
own 32-pass cap).
.TP
.I t17
-
.I t19
Minimal composition (no attachments): headers present with no
stdin body, an ASCII stdin body printed 7bit/US-ASCII, and a body
with one high-bit byte switching to 8bit/UTF-8.
.TP
.I t20
-
.I t21
Attachments: a directly-displayable
.RB ( .txt )
attachment passes through verbatim inside its own MIME part; an
attachment with no recognized extension is base64-encoded and
decoded back byte-for-byte against the original.
.TP
.I t22
A high-bit
.B -s
subject is RFC2047 Q-encoded.
.TP
.I t23
-
.I t25
An unknown flag, no recipients given at all, and attaching a
nonexistent file each fail cleanly.
.TP
.I t26
The user's own default-headers file
.RB ( $home/mail/headers )
is read regardless of
.BR -n ,
and a custom header there suppresses the matching auto-generated
one - the real gap this suite's own development caught by re-reading
the source, not by a test failing (see
.IR marshal (1)'s
own BUGS).
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/marshaltest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR marshal (1)
.SH BUGS
Does not re-verify the address engine's own oracle-derived
expectations against a live, automatically-built oracle every run,
unlike
.IR qrtest (1)
or
.IR cbtest (1)
- that harness needed the rest of
.B upas/common
merely to link (mbox paths, folder locking, none of which the address
engine itself calls), which would have made this suite markedly less
reproducible for a piece of verification that does not need
re-demonstrating on every run. The expectations it produced are
recorded as comments alongside each case in the source instead.
.PP
Does not exercise
.BR -8 " (recipients taken from stdin's own To:/Cc:/Bcc: lines rather"
than
.IR recipient-list ),
.BR -C / -B
(Cc:/Bcc: recipients),
.BR -R
.RI ( In-Reply-To: ),
or
.B -t
(forcing an attachment's MIME type) - each is a straightforward,
independently-traceable path through code the other cases already
exercise, not a gap discovered by trying and failing.
.PP
Does not exercise
.IR marshal (1)'s
own CLI entry point
.RB ( init() 's
own stdin-reading and stdout-writing) - every case here drives
.BR run() 's
own
.I data
byte array directly, matching this backlog's own convention.
